**Verdict:** trust intact, value high, recommendation **revise** — 0 blockers, 7 concerns. Duke Dennis would recognize himself here: the portrait runs on his own verified words, respects his privacy boundaries, handles Union Square proportionately, and honestly holds the one fact the typing can't explain. But all seven concerns lean the same direction — the draft repeatedly sharpens him one degree past what the tape supports.

**Strongest hit (SUBJECT-H1):** "His mother's sentence outlasted his own" — the draft presents the stayed-four-years paradox as unexplained rather than forcing it into the Type 8 frame, which is the hardest fairness test a typing piece faces.

**Highest-priority concern (SUBJECT-C1):** "He ruined a generation" is rendered as Duke's flat assessment of Kai, but on the 2024 tape it's Speedy quoting Duke's older line back to him — Duke's first response is "I wouldn't say ruined it," and he only repeats it when pressed. The draft applies to Duke exactly the words-in-my-mouth dynamic the article says he resents; the fix is one clause framing it as a bit he's said, walked back, and re-upped.

My two research passes confirmed no long-form self-account exists after the Feb 2024 Complex interview, so that tape governs all present-tense claims — which is why the TL;DR's "still calls enlisting the worst decision of his life" (C3) also needs softening to match his 2024 stance.

